          "ruleId": "CWE-345: Insufficient Verification of Data Authenticity",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Tag-triggered release builds arbitrary unverified commit  [CVSS 9.0: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H]"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": ".github/workflows/build.yml"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 3
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "critical",
            "security-severity": "9.0",
            "cvssRating": "Critical",
            "category": "CWE-345: Insufficient Verification of Data Authenticity",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H",
            "cwe": "CWE-345 - Insufficient Verification of Data Authenticity",
            "cweId": "CWE-345",
            "cweName": "Insufficient Verification of Data Authenticity",
            "cvssScore": 9.0,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *internal-network position required*",
            "confidence": 0.82,
            "description": "#### Description\nThe workflow triggers on tags matching 'v*' (line 7). When a tag is pushed, actions/checkout@v4 (line 27) checks out the source at the tag's target commit with no verification that this commit is reachable from main or master. The build job compiles whatever code is at that commit (lines 34-50), packages it into WhisperNote.exe and WhisperNote-win-x64.zip (lines 52-97), and the release job (lines 116-146) with contents:write permission publishes them as a GitHub Release via gh release create. There is no step that validates the tag's SHA is on the main/master branch, no use of a protected branch ref, and no environment gate. This means any commit in the repository \u2014 including one on an unmerged PR branch or a detached commit \u2014 can be tagged and released. The SHA-pinned actions prevent compromise of the actions themselves but do not constrain which source code is built.\n\n#### Impact\nAn attacker with tag push access to the repository can point a v* tag at a commit containing a backdoored WhisperNote.csproj or injected binary. The workflow compiles and publishes that code as an official GitHub Release, delivering RCE to every user who downloads and runs the release artifact. The release job's contents:write permission makes the malicious artifact publicly distributed.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker with repository write access creates a branch containing a malicious WhisperNote.csproj that downloads a reverse shell on first launch. They push tag v99.9.9 pointing to that commit. The workflow triggers on the tag event, checks out the malicious commit, compiles the backdoored WhisperNote.exe, and the release job publishes it as a GitHub Release. All users who download and execute the release artifact are compromised with code execution on their Windows hosts.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker has push access to the repository sufficient to create and push a tag\n- Repository does not enforce a branch protection rule requiring v* tags to target commits on main/master\n- A user downloads and executes the released WhisperNote.exe or extracts the ZIP\n\n```\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main, master]\n    tags:\n      - 'v*'\n    paths:\n      - whisper-note/**\n      - .github/workflows/build.yml\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nAdd a verification step at the start of the build job (before checkout or immediately after) that resolves the tag's target commit and confirms it is reachable from the main or master branch. For example, after checkout run: git merge-base --is-ancestor HEAD origin/main || exit 1. Alternatively, restrict the tag trigger to require the tag to be created via a protected workflow that only fires on verified main/master SHAs, or require a deployment environment gate on the release job that an authorized maintainer must manually approve.\n\n**Exploitability:** Likely CVSS 9.x-class because tag-triggered builds can publish arbitrary unverified commits as release artifacts with no provenance, signing, branch reachability, or environment gate. The primitive is malicious release artifact production and distribution, a high-impact supply-chain result. It is especially dangerous when combined with unsigned release consumption.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 workflow builds and publishes as a GitHub Release (contents:write, gh release create) whatever commit a v* tag points to, with no ancestry check, environment gate, or other provenance validation; a repo write-access holder can ship arbitrary code to end users."
          },
