          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unversioned npx mcp-remote@latest enables supply-chain RCE  [CVSS 8.5: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H]"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": ".codex/config.toml"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 1
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "high",
            "security-severity": "8.5",
            "cvssRating": "High",
            "category": "CWE-829: Inclusion of Functionality from Untrusted Control Sphere",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H",
            "cwe": "CWE-829 - Inclusion of Functionality from Untrusted Control Sphere",
            "cweId": "CWE-829",
            "cweName": "Inclusion of Functionality from Untrusted Control Sphere",
            "cvssScore": 8.5,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.93,
            "description": "#### Description\nThe .codex/config.toml registers the Atlassian-Rovo-MCP server with command 'npx' and args ['-y', 'mcp-remote@latest', 'https://mcp.atlassian.com/v1/mcp/authv2']. The @latest spec means no version is pinned, and the -y flag auto-confirms installation without any user prompt. The factory's CodexAgentExecutor.run() (factory/src/codex.ts:107-111) spawns the Codex CLI with cwd set to config.repoPath, with an explicit comment: 'Keep the process rooted at the repository so Codex loads the existing .codex/config.toml MCP registration.' When any Jira operation is invoked (McpJiraAdapter.structuredUnqueued at mcp-jira.ts:175 sets toolScope to AgentToolScope.Jira), the MCP server is not disabled (the disable logic at codex.ts:90-92 only fires for AgentToolScope.Build), so Codex launches 'npx -y mcp-remote@latest' which downloads and executes the latest mcp-remote code from the npm registry. The runtimeEnv() method (codex.ts:50-55) spreads the entire process.env into the child process, meaning the mcp-remote process inherits every secret in the factory's environment. The factory performs Jira operations (searchReady, getIssue, transition, addComment) as part of its automatic run loop, so the malicious package is fetched and executed on every run cycle without user interaction.\n\n#### Impact\nA remote attacker who compromises the mcp-remote npm package (maintainer takeover, registry compromise, or malicious @latest publish) achieves arbitrary code execution on every developer's workstation with full access to all environment variables (GitHub tokens, Jira API keys, LLM provider keys) and the entire repository filesystem. The factory runs Jira operations automatically, so exploitation requires no user action beyond normal factory operation.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker compromises the mcp-remote npm package maintainer account and publishes a malicious version tagged as latest. The malicious postinstall script exfiltrates all environment variables (GITHUB_TOKEN, JIRA_API_TOKEN, LLM API keys) to an attacker-controlled endpoint and drops a persistent backdoor. The next time the factory's automatic Jira polling loop calls searchReady() or getIssue(), Codex reads .codex/config.toml, executes 'npx -y mcp-remote@latest', and the malicious code runs with full host access. All developer workstations using this repository are compromised simultaneously.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Factory performs a Jira operation (core automatic function of the run loop)\n- Attacker compromises the mcp-remote npm package or npm registry\n\n```\n[mcp_servers.Atlassian-Rovo-MCP]\ncommand = \"npx\"\nargs = [\"-y\", \"mcp-remote@latest\", \"https://mcp.atlassian.com/v1/mcp/authv2\"]\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nPin mcp-remote to a specific immutable version in .codex/config.toml (e.g., 'mcp-remote@1.2.3') and verify the package via a lockfile or integrity hash before execution. Remove the -y flag so that version changes require explicit operator confirmation. Alternatively, install mcp-remote as a local node_modules dependency with a committed package-lock.json so the factory never fetches remote code at runtime. At minimum, restrict the environment variables passed to MCP server processes in runtimeEnv() (codex.ts:50-55) to only those required for MCP operation, limiting credential exposure if the bridge is compromised.\n\n**Exploitability:** Likely CVSS 8.8-9.4-class depending on scoring of privilege and interaction: unversioned npx -y mcp-remote@latest is fetched and executed automatically during Jira factory runs, with the full process environment inherited. This gives npm-registry or MCP-endpoint compromise a direct local RCE primitive and a convenient path to steal local secrets. It is a high-value chain node because it can be triggered through normal Jira workflow operations.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 8/10) \u2014 Unversioned `npx -y mcp-remote@latest` in .codex/config.toml is auto-loaded on the default CodexMcp Jira path (toolScop\u2026"
          },
