          "ruleId": "CWE-494",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unverified GitHub release binaries overwrite local llama.cpp  [CVSS 7.5: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H]"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "llm-servers/scripts/update-github-release.ps1"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 172
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "high",
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "cvssRating": "High",
            "category": "CWE-494",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
            "cwe": "CWE-494",
            "cweId": "CWE-494",
            "cvssScore": 7.5,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.6,
            "description": "#### Description\nExternal input enters at line 68 (Invoke-RestMethod against api.github.com release feed) and line 172 ($Asset.browser_download_url taken verbatim from that feed). Lines 69-75 select the most recently published prerelease matching the asset regex; the only 'up to date' decision (line 149) tests whether a marker for the latest feed tag exists locally \u2014 there is no comparison of the candidate TagName (line 136) with $CurrentVersion (line 105), so a newer local build is overwritten with an older one. Line 181 downloads with `curl.exe -sL` (no --fail, no size or hash check). Line 198 runs Expand-Archive with no validation of entry paths (Windows PowerShell 5.1 has historically mishandled '../' entries). Lines 228-246 then copy every top-level file into InstallDir; the MD5 compare at lines 232-233 only skips byte-identical files \u2014 it is a dedupe, not an integrity check. No SHA256, Authenticode, or version pin exists anywhere between the API response and the overwrite. The installed binaries are subsequently executed by the start scripts (e.g., llm-servers/llama/windows/scripts/start-dflash.ps1:6, llm-servers/beellama/scripts/start-Q5-kvarn4-120k-vision.ps1:6).\n\n#### Impact\nA malicious or compromised prerelease asset from the upstream GitHub repo is downloaded, extracted, and copied over the local inference binaries with zero authenticity verification, so the next server start executes attacker code with the developer's privileges. The same state machine never compares TagName against the local VERSION marker, so it also silently downgrades a newer local install to whatever the feed currently reports.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker who gains the ability to publish releases to ggml-org/llama.cpp or Anbeeld/beellama.cpp ships a prerelease whose asset name matches '^llama-.*-bin-win-cuda-13.*-x64\\.zip$' and contains a trojanized llama-server.exe. A developer runs the documented update-llama.ps1; the script picks that prerelease as 'latest', downloads it, expands it, and overwrites llama/windows/llama/llama-server.exe, writing VERSION-<tag> (line 256-257) so all future runs report 'already up to date'. The developer's next start-*.ps1 execution runs the trojan with full local privileges.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- attacker controls release publishing on the upstream GitHub repo (or the GitHub platform)\n- developer runs the update script, which is the documented workflow\n- developer later starts the installed server binary\n\n```\n$DownloadUrl = $Asset.browser_download_url\n...\n$null = curl.exe -sL -o $TempZip -H \"User-Agent: $UserAgent\" $DownloadUrl\n...\nExpand-Archive -Path $TempZip -DestinationPath $TempDir -Force\n...\n$HashSrc = Get-FileHash $File.FullName -Algorithm MD5\n$HashDst = Get-FileHash $Dest         -Algorithm MD5\n...\nCopy-Item -Path $File.FullName -Destination $Dest -Force\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nThe update pipeline must authenticate artifacts before overwrite: verify the downloaded asset's SHA256 against a pinned, out-of-band per-tag manifest (or validate Authenticode signatures) in Update-GitHubRelease before the Copy-Item block at lines 228-246; refuse to downgrade by comparing $TagName with $CurrentVersion before the line 149 early-exit; use `curl.exe --fail --max-filesize <Asset.size>` at line 181; and reject zip entries containing '..' or absolute paths before Expand-Archive at line 198.\n\n**Exploitability:** Likely CVSS 7-8-class: unverified GitHub release download and archive extraction overwrite local llama.cpp binaries, which are later executed by start scripts. The primitive is remote code substitution leading to local code execution when the server starts. It is a strong standalone host-compromise path and also a natural follow-on to malicious release or tag workflows.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 8/10) \u2014 no hash/pin/signature or version comparison exists between the GitHub feed and the binary overwrite that start scripts then execute; downgrade path confirmed in so\u2026"
          },
