          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "medium",
            "security-severity": "5.4",
            "cvssRating": "Medium",
            "category": "CWE-829: Inclusion of Functionality from Untrusted Control Sphere",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:C/C:L/I:L/A:N",
            "cwe": "CWE-829 - Inclusion of Functionality from Untrusted Control Sphere",
            "cweId": "CWE-829",
            "cweName": "Inclusion of Functionality from Untrusted Control Sphere",
            "cvssScore": 5.4,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.95,
            "description": "#### Description\nLine 43 references the external action as `anomalyco/opencode/github@latest`. The `@latest` tag is a mutable, unversioned reference: GitHub resolves it to the newest tag in the anomalyco/opencode repository at execution time. No SHA pin, no digest, and no integrity check exists between the reference and the code that actually runs. The step on lines 41-67 injects three secrets into that external action's environment: OPENAI_API_KEY (line 45), OPENAI_BASE_URL (line 46), and GITHUB_TOKEN (line 63). The job-level permissions block (lines 16-19) grants the GITHUB_TOKEN contents:read, issues:write, and pull-requests:write. The `use_github_token: true` parameter on line 67 further confirms the action is expected to use the token for API calls. If an attacker publishes a new version under `@latest`\u2014via account takeover, a malicious maintainer, a compromised CI in the opencode repo, or a dependency-confusion attack against the action's own build\u2014the next `ai-review`-labelled PR triggers execution of the attacker's code with all three secrets available in-process. `persist-credentials: false` (line 24) and the scoped permissions block mitigate but do not prevent this: the secrets are still passed directly into the untrusted action's environment.\n\n#### Impact\nA compromise of the anomalyco GitHub account or the opencode repository allows an attacker to push a malicious action version that every subsequent workflow run will execute. The attacker gains OPENAI_API_KEY, a GITHUB_TOKEN with issues:write and pull-requests:write, and read access to the repository checkout, enabling secret exfiltration, injection of fabricated review comments, and manipulation of issues/PRs in the target repo.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker compromises the `anomalyco` GitHub account (credential leak, MFA fatigue, or a malicious PR merged into the opencode repo). They push a new action version tagged `latest` whose entrypoint reads `process.env.OPENAI_API_KEY` and `process.env.GITHUB_TOKEN`, exfiltrates both to an attacker-controlled endpoint, and uses the GITHUB_TOKEN to open a tracking issue in the target repository confirming the compromise. The next developer labels a PR `ai-review`; the workflow resolves `@latest` to the malicious version and executes it, leaking the API key and granting the attacker write access to issues and PRs for the duration of the run.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker must compromise the anomalyco GitHub account or the opencode repository (account takeover, malicious maintainer, or supply-chain attack on the action's own build pipeline)\n- A pull request must be labelled `ai-review` after the malicious version is published, triggering the workflow\n\n```\nuses: anomalyco/opencode/github@latest\n  env:\n    OPENAI_API_KEY: ${{ secrets.OPENAI_API_KEY }}\n    OPENAI_BASE_URL: ${{ secrets.OPENAI_BASE_URL }}\n    ...\n    GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}\n  with:\n    model: llamacpp/unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-UD-Q5_K_XL\n    variant: medium\n    use_github_token: true\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nReplace the mutable `@latest` reference on line 43 with a full 40-character commit SHA (e.g., `uses: anomalyco/opencode/github@9a8b7c6d...`), verified against a trusted release in the opencode repository. Add a periodic audit step (or Dependabot/Renovate rule) that alerts when the pinned SHA drifts from the latest upstream release so the team can consciously re-pin after review. Consider moving OPENAI_API_KEY out of the action's environment and instead passing it via a short-lived, scoped credential that the action cannot exfiltrate to arbitrary hosts.\n\n**Exploitability:** Likely CVSS 7-8-class: @latest external GitHub Action receives OPENAI_API_KEY, OPENAI_BASE_URL, and GITHUB_TOKEN, with scoped but meaningful issues/PR write permissions. The main primitive is supply-chain action compromise leading to secret access and repository manipulation. It is less immediate than direct PowerShell or npm RCE, but very dangerous in AI review workflows and a\u2026"
          },
