            "category": "CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:L",
            "cwe": "CWE-306 - Missing Authentication for Critical Function",
            "cweId": "CWE-306",
            "cweName": "Missing Authentication for Critical Function",
            "cvssScore": 6.5,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *internal-network position required*",
            "confidence": 0.72,
            "description": "#### Description\nRemoteExecutionServer.StartAsync binds a TcpListener to the operator-supplied RemoteListenEndpoint with zero authentication. The endpoint is validated only by AppSettings.TryNormalizeHttpListenEndpoint, which accepts ANY http:// host (it does not require 127.0.0.1/localhost). Once bound, every accepted request flows into ProcessRemoteRequestAsync (MainWindowViewModel.cs:384), which has no caller identity check \u2014 the only guard is that the active provider must be Local. The request body (raw PCM) is passed straight into ServerManager.TranscribeAsync and the resulting transcript string is written back to the requester. In addition, when _state.RemoteSettingsControlEnabled is true, an attacker request reaches ApplyRemoteSettingsAsync (MainWindowViewModel.cs:413), which writes new AutoOffloadVram/ThinkingEnabled values into local AppState and the running server via ServerManager.ApplyRemoteSettingsAsync, and then pushes them onto the WPF dispatcher. Because there is no shared secret, token, or origin check between the operator's config and the accepted connection, the 'remote' trust boundary is defined purely by network reachability. The threat model lists 'CI secrets and API keys' and 'Local LLM inference service availability' as critical/high assets; this endpoint gives an adjacent-network actor both inference compute and transcript exfiltration for free.\n\n#### Impact\nAny process or host that can reach the TCP listener can submit arbitrary audio bytes and receive transcribed text, and when RemoteSettingsControlEnabled is on, can also mutate AutoOffloadVram and ThinkingEnabled on the host. This exposes user speech content to any adjacent network principal and lets a non-operator principal flip application behavior, directly degrading the local inference service and host integrity.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker on the same LAN (or any host that can route to the operator machine) discovers the WhisperNote remote-execution port (default is an HTTP port chosen in settings; TryNormalizeHttpListenEndpoint will happily accept http://0.0.0.0:<port>). They send POST /api/transcriptions with a captured or synthesized PCM payload. The server transcribes it locally and returns the text, leaking whatever speech the operator recorded or allowing the attacker to burn GPU inference. If the operator also enabled 'Remote settings control', the attacker additionally POSTs /api/settings with {\"autoOffloadVram\":false,\"thinkingEnabled\":true} to persistently change the app's behavior. No credential is required at any step.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- RemoteServerEnabled must be true in WhisperNote settings (opt-in feature)\n- RemoteListenEndpoint must resolve to an address reachable by the attacker (the normalizer allows any http host, including 0.0.0.0 or a LAN IP)\n- For settings mutation: RemoteSettingsControlEnabled must also be true\n\n```\nvar address = await ResolveListenAddressAsync(endpoint.Host);\nlistener = new TcpListener(address, endpoint.Port);\nlistener.Start();\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nRequire a per-instance shared secret (e.g. a random 256-bit token generated on first run, stored in the local state file, and sent by the client in an Authorization header or query parameter) and reject any request that does not present it. Additionally, in AppSettings.TryNormalizeHttpListenEndpoint, restrict accepted listen endpoints to loopback (127.0.0.1 / [::1] / localhost) unless an explicit opt-in to non-loopback binding is provided, and enforce that check in RemoteExecutionServer.StartAsync before calling listener.Start().\n\n**Exploitability:** Likely CVSS 7-8-class: an unauthenticated TCP transcription server can be triggered by any reachable host, allowing free inference use, transcript exfiltration, and remote state mutation when remote settings are enabled. The primitive is data theft, denial of service, and limited local configuration tampering. It is not by itself a code-execution bug, but it is a strong lateral foothold and data-leak amp\u2026"
          },
