          "ruleId": "CWE-250",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Factory test workflow grants GITHUB_TOKEN default (potentially write) repo permissions  [CVSS 6.8: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:C/C:N/I:H/A:N]"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": ".github/workflows/factory.yml"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 17
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "medium",
            "security-severity": "6.8",
            "cvssRating": "Medium",
            "category": "CWE-250",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:C/C:N/I:H/A:N",
            "cwe": "CWE-250",
            "cweId": "CWE-250",
            "cvssScore": 6.8,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.72,
            "description": "#### Description\nThe `test` job (lines 17-35) runs on `windows-latest` and executes `npm ci --prefix factory` (line 32) followed by `npm test --prefix factory` (line 35). Both commands can execute arbitrary JavaScript: `npm ci` runs `preinstall`/`postinstall`/`prepare` lifecycle scripts of every dependency, and `npm test` runs the project's test suite. Neither step is sandboxed. The job has no `permissions:` key, so the GITHUB_TOKEN that GitHub Actions injects into the environment carries whatever default permission level the repository owner has configured under Settings \u2192 Actions \u2192 Workflow permissions. The build.yml workflow in the same repository correctly scopes its build job to `contents: read` (build.yml line 23), demonstrating the pattern that is absent here. If the repo default is write, the token can call the GitHub API to create commits, push refs, open PRs, and modify branch protection rules\u2014giving a compromised npm dependency a full repository-write primitive with no additional authentication.\n\n#### Impact\nBecause the `test` job declares no `permissions:` block, the GITHUB_TOKEN available to every step inherits the repository's default workflow permission level. If the repo is configured for \"Read and write\" (a common setting on older or private repos), the token can push branches, open PRs, and modify repository settings. A malicious npm package installed by `npm ci` (line 32) or a hostile test executed by `npm test` (line 35) can read the token from `process.env.GITHUB_TOKEN` and use it to push a backdoored commit to main, bypassing code review entirely.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker publishes a malicious version of a transitive dependency in the factory/npm ecosystem (e.g., a typosquatted or compromised package already in `factory/package-lock.json`). A maintainer merges a dependency update. The next `factory.yml` run on push triggers `npm ci`, which installs the malicious package and executes its `postinstall` script. The script reads `process.env.GITHUB_TOKEN`, makes a `POST /repos/{owner}/{repo}/git/refs` API call to push a commit to `main` that adds a credential-exfiltration script to the factory CLI entrypoint, then creates a PR labeled \"chore: bump lockfile\" to look routine. The PR is merged (or the push is direct if branch protection allows it), and the backdoor is now in the production factory code.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Repository's default workflow permissions are set to \"Read and write\" (or the job inherits elevated permissions)\n- Attacker compromises or poisons an npm package in the factory dependency tree\n- A push or PR to factory/ triggers the workflow (routine development activity)\n\n```\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: windows-latest\n\n    steps:\n      - name: Checkout\n        uses: actions/checkout@11d5960a326750d5838078e36cf38b85af677262 # v4\n      ...\n      - name: Install dependencies\n        run: npm ci --prefix factory\n\n      - name: Run factory tests\n        run: npm test --prefix factory\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nAdd an explicit `permissions:` block to the `test` job at line 18, scoped to the minimum required: `permissions: { contents: read }`. This mirrors the pattern already used in build.yml (line 23) and ensures that even if a dependency runs arbitrary code during `npm ci` or `npm test`, the GITHUB_TOKEN it can access cannot write to the repository, create issues, or modify settings. If the test suite genuinely needs to interact with the GitHub API (e.g., integration tests against the GitHub API), scope the token to `contents: read, pull-requests: read` and use a dedicated fine-grained PAT for any write operations.\n\n**Exploitability:** Likely CVSS 4-6-class: the factory test workflow lacks a permissions block, so GITHUB_TOKEN inherits repository defaults. If those defaults are broad, lifecycle scripts or test code can become a path to repository write. The primitive is privilege overreach and potential CI-to-repo escalation, but it depends on the repo\u2019s default token configuration.\n\n#### Adv\u2026"
